WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.129
Film Courage: Going back to Author Philip
Roth again, seeing interviews with him toward

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:10.400
the later years of his life he had moved from
New York City to the Connecticut woods to

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.400
be left alone.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.630
All of the journalist [who came to his new
residence] said “Isn’t it lonely here

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:14.630
for you?”

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.449
And he said “It is but I enjoy it.

00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:18.869
There is no friction, there’s nothing.”

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:26.039
Because I guess it was after his book Portnoy’s
Complaint that he was receiving so much attention

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:30.349
and was bombarded with people’s opinions
and this was simply an easier way for him

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:31.349
to continue writing.

00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:32.810
I realize this is a common thing.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:38.340
Sort of taking one’s self off of the map
so you can create but the loneliness was worth

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.890
it versus the friction.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:43.270
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer:&nbsp; Yes and
obviously it worked for him because other

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:49.220
people who would go off to live in the woods
end up not being productive because they think

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.420
that’s going to solve their problem.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.570
I learned this the hard way because I had
to finish a book early in my academic career

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.940
and I decided I’d go to my parent’s lake
house cottage for the summer and just sit

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.670
there and finish it.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:07.490
And of course I almost got nothing done that
summer because one thing led to the other,

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:12.710
people would stop by to visit because there
was the lake and the lawn would need attending

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.219
or the cabin itself needed fixing.

00:01:15.219 --> 00:01:20.750
I used every excuse I could possibly think
of to avoid sitting down to write.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:27.289
This is where I worked out a lot of the theories
that are in my view of creativity is that

00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:33.350
summer because pressure is what causes creativity
to work best.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.950
Lack of pressure actually works against you.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:42.340
So as a producer I’d much rather have a
low-budget film to deal with with every single

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.350
thing that you do has to be a solution to
the fact that you don’t have enough money

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:51.450
to do it so it becomes more creative and you
tell the crew that.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.979
We have to have creative solutions to these
issues because money is not going to solve

00:01:55.979 --> 00:01:56.979
this.

00:01:56.979 --> 00:01:58.380
We don’t have the money.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.380
And of course studio films don’t have that
issue.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.880
They have endless and so on.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.970
But nonetheless you can see that if there
was more discipline to them a lot of them

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.100
would be better than they are.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:16.239
When you see a film that has six or seven
writers listed you know at the beginning as

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:20.310
screenwriters you know that this was just
caused by money.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.599
They didn’t work with writer number three
long enough.

00:02:23.599 --> 00:02:28.489
They just fired him and brought in writer
number four and that was the expensive way

00:02:28.489 --> 00:02:30.790
to do it.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:35.900
But there’s a challenge in the pressure
that comes and time pressure is the number

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:41.739
one pressure more than financial even that
works on behalf of creativity if you only

00:02:41.739 --> 00:02:44.700
have a limited amount of time.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:50.620
I always found that I did my most creative
work a half an hour before a committee meeting

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.680
because I hated committee meetings.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:59.230
And I still find that if I have to go to something
that I’m not wild about going to, I’m

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:03.099
suddenly extremely creative an hour before
that.

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:10.201
Rather than resenting that, I schedule my
creativity around that so that that’s when

00:03:10.201 --> 00:03:13.650
I do it whenever I can.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:17.689
I think that that’s what we have to learn
about our minds is how to kind of trick them

00:03:17.689 --> 00:03:22.980
into behaving the way we want them to behave
to producing what we want them to produce.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.760
Film Courage: You’ve talked about the Type-C
Personality and then in your book How To Escape

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.459
Lifetime Security and Pursue Your Impossible
Dream - A Guide To Transforming Your Career…is

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:37.640
it Chapter 6 “A Day In The Life Of Type-C.”
I’m wondering if we can talk about this.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.571
What is a day in the life for Type-C?

00:03:39.571 --> 00:03:40.900
Is it a structured day?

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:51.500
Dr. Atchity: Well it’s different for every
Type-C and it’s going to be different from

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.319
people who are not Type-C’s.

00:03:53.319 --> 00:04:03.200
And how it’s different is that the Type-C
has learned how to arrange his day to fit

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.969
his type, to fit his or her mind.

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:14.879
Some people are night owls and some people
are early birds and the early bird writer

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:18.199
is not going to write late at night.

00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:22.500
She is not comfortable writing late at night,
she’s comfortable in the morning.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:28.880
So if she gets up at 4:00 a.m. she’s going
to give herself, as much time as she has the

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.480
attention span for to do her writing in the
morning.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:39.740
Which is when I love to do mine because no
one interrupts you from 4 to 7 in the morning.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:44.880
But if you’re a night owl as Tolkien was,
he wrote LORD OF THE RINGS completely after

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:51.030
1:00 a.m. at night, because he was so busy
all of the time before then and had a family

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:55.150
and everything else so he wrote in the middle
of the night.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:05:01.650
Sometimes he wrote all night and just went
off to school to teach without any sleep at

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:02.650
all.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.310
But that was okay because he was doing what
he loved.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:11.169
So his day would be arranged differently than
the day of someone who is on a clock where

00:05:11.169 --> 00:05:12.370
it’s not their clock.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:18.270
Someone who has to show up for a nine o’clock
job is not on their own clock and their day

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:22.810
is going to be one that probably they are
upset with most of the time.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:27.960
Whereas if you’re a Type-C and you’re
in charge of your own life, you’re going

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:34.290
arrange it around the patterns that work best
for your mind and and that is a crucial part

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:39.120
of becoming a Type-C is having your own kind
of day.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:46.870
I go to meetings to sell the properties that
we’ve developed and I don’t like going

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:52.400
to meetings because it takes a lot of time
to get there and once you’re there there

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.610
is a certain amount of wasted time and then
once you do the thing that’s always fun

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.400
(even though you dreaded it).

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:04.410
So I try to arrange my day so that I’m doing
something that is very productive.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.880
Like I always say I didn’t get any work
done and my wife is telling me “What are

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:08.880
you talking about?

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.000
You went to three pitch meetings at three
different networks?”

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.669
Yeah, I know but I don’t feel like I got
any work done, it’s just a mental view of

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:21.259
things.

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:27.699
So I think every Type-C day is going to be
different and what you really need to do is

00:06:27.699 --> 00:06:32.460
if you’re interested in pursuing this for
yourself is you need to figure out what is

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.870
your ideal day.

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.740
Is it important to you to go for a walk?

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.760
Is it important to you to meditate?

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:43.900
Is it important to you to spend X time on
your creative work?

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.949
And is it important for you to spend X time
with your family and all of those things?

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:53.190
And you sit there and rearrange your day to
make that work, that’s what time management

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:54.460
is all about.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:57.060
And how do you do it?

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.060
No matter how busy you are there are busier
people.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.330
I was reading Michelle Obama’s book and
nobody could be busier than the President

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:08.810
of The United States and the First Lady of
The United States yet some how they made time

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:14.980
for everything they needed to make time for
which tells you there was time management

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:16.389
at work.

00:07:16.389 --> 00:07:21.389
Because certainly if anybody had infinite
things to do or infinite work to do it would

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:22.990
be those two.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:25.520
But if they can do it, you can do it, too.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:29.909
Film Courage: I think I read that Philip K.
Dick liked to write at night and he would

00:07:29.909 --> 00:07:33.930
stay up all night (I’m not sure if some
of it was maybe chemically induced).

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:40.289
But then when he remarried his new wife wanted
him to write from 9-to-5.

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:44.330
She said “I’m very middle-class, bourgeois
and I like these hours.”

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.470
So he eventually got his own apartment or
space that he called The Hovel and it was

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:55.909
dirty but he felt he did his best writing
there in this dirty space and it lent to what

00:07:55.909 --> 00:07:57.300
he was doing.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:04.479
Ken: Yes…the “hovel” syndrome is interesting
because I think every creative person can

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:06.350
relate to that.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:12.289
President Obama called his “The Hole”
and it was always a room that could be found

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:14.460
in any house that they were in.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.789
Where nothing could be touched, he could do
whatever he wanted with it and usually there

00:08:18.789 --> 00:08:22.720
were papers all over the floor and it was
there that he finished a book or a speech

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.099
and so on.

00:08:24.099 --> 00:08:25.729
And The Hovel is the same idea.

00:08:25.729 --> 00:08:32.870
I’ve always noticed that by the weekend
my office is a complete mess, there are things

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:33.870
all over the floor.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:38.700
And then by Monday it’s all ship shape.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.250
When you think about that it’s nothing but
the externalization of the creative process

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:47.620
because the creative process is making order
out of chaos.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:52.380
In Saint John’s Gospel it says “In the
beginning was the word and the word was with

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.140
God and the word was God and he was the beginning
with God.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.010
All things were made through him.”

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:02.490
And he goes on and talks about the light “Let
there be light.”

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:03.490
Etcetera.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:09.310
So when the artist creates something, he is
taking a bunch of little things and creating

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:15.120
order out of them and so the externalized
version of that is living in a messy place

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.750
and straightening it up when as much as you
have to whenever you have to.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:26.440
And if there is some external force that is
forcing you to straighten it up then that

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:29.620
creative person is not in charge of their
own life.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:33.680
And they can be, you can always find a way
to do it.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:40.130
There’s a touching short story by Doris
Lessing ‘To Room Nineteen’ (I think that’s

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:41.130
the name of it?

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:42.680
To Room Sixteen, maybe?)

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.380
In any case it’s one of her most greatest
short stories.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:52.760
It’s about a house wife who longed all of
her life to have a room of her own and it

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:58.279
was because she couldn’t be herself in her
family and she couldn’t do what she wanted

00:09:58.279 --> 00:10:04.640
to do and she didn’t feel free and I won’t
tell you how it ends because it’s not a

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:05.640
fun ending.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:12.300
But it’s a very tragic example of what happens
if you don’t take charge of your own creative

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.839
life.

00:10:13.839 --> 00:10:22.490
Interestingly Tolkien wrote a very introspective
piece called ‘Leaf By Niggle.’

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:30.410
Strange title but Niggle was the name of painter
who had this amazing vision of this spectacular

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:37.101
forest and his vision was so clear that he
could see every tree in the forest, every

00:10:37.101 --> 00:10:42.930
animal in the forest clearly, every leaf on
every tree in the forest and because he was

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:48.089
so busy he never got around to painting more
than a single leaf.

00:10:48.089 --> 00:10:52.130
That’s the way the story ends up.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:57.769
And it’s really Tolkien’s agonized argument
for why he had to write in the middle of the

00:10:57.769 --> 00:11:02.650
night because he determined that he was not
going to be Niggle.

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:08.310
Even though he wrote something like 40 books
on linguistics and different languages and

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:14.990
of course…Lord Of The Rings and The Silmarillion
and many other great works he felt that he

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:22.580
had barely gotten to one tree in his forest
and only that because he wrote all night.

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:29.910
So that is a terrible thing to kind of carry
around is the belief that you can do amazing

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:31.970
thing which you don’t have time to do them.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:35.500
And the answer is, that’s not right, you
do have time.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.010
I mean where did Michelangelo find his time?

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:40.779
Where did Leonardo da Vinci find his time?

00:11:40.779 --> 00:11:47.829
They all had the exact same number of hours
that we have and your job is to take your

00:11:47.829 --> 00:11:50.950
vision seriously and find those hours to make
it happen.

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:54.980
Film Courage: Or someone like [Author] Alice
Munro when she first started out was I guess

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:59.149
raising four children and she didn’t want
the other housewives in the neighborhood to

00:11:59.149 --> 00:12:04.110
know that she was a writer because she thought
that she would get the “weird” label,

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:09.130
which she ended up getting and she didn’t
care anyway but…I guess when you win a Nobel

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:13.180
Prize it takes all of that away.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:17.550
But she would [write] when the children were
napping and if the other housewives knocked

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:19.680
on the door she would put all of her writings
away.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.810
She didn’t want people to know.

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:24.889
I realize that stigma is probably no longer
today…

00:12:24.889 --> 00:12:28.610
Dr. Atchity: No, it’s still there.

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:35.389
It originates in people’s families and it’s
when you announce to your father or your mother

00:12:35.389 --> 00:12:38.980
that you’re going to be a writer or you
are going to be a circus clown or you are

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:42.730
going to be a dancer or you are going to be
an actress.

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:49.990
And that is where it starts because the normal
response is “What are you going to do for

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:50.990
a living?”

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:52.920
And that haunts you.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:58.339
There’s another one of my books where I
talk about learning (as you go into the creative

00:12:58.339 --> 00:13:04.070
life) learning who your true friends are and
learning who your friendly associates are

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:09.329
because you lose most of your friendly associates
when you make a decision to go from a rational

00:13:09.329 --> 00:13:11.910
life to a creative life.

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:17.889
I once gave a regular class at UCLA that was
called Keeping Your Spirits Up For Creative

00:13:17.889 --> 00:13:18.889
People.

00:13:18.889 --> 00:13:23.949
And one time there were a bunch of actresses
in the class and at the beginning of the class

00:13:23.949 --> 00:13:29.910
I said “Let’s go around the circle and
everyone introduce themselves and tell me

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:36.660
your name and where you are from and what
is the worst question that you could be asked

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:42.079
at a bar or a cocktail party in Los Angeles
and how do you respond to it?”

00:13:42.079 --> 00:13:49.800
And one lady said one lady said she was from
Arkansas and her name was Jo and the worst

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:54.241
question that she had in LA was “When are
you going to go back to Arkansas and work

00:13:54.241 --> 00:13:56.600
in the Post Office again?”

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:58.230
And I said “And how do you answer that?

00:13:58.230 --> 00:13:59.230
That’s terrible!”

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:03.830
And she goes “Usually by bursting into tears
and leaving the room.”

00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:08.480
And I said “Well, hopefully this class will
find some help for that.”

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:16.490
The next woman said her name was Jenny and
she was from California and she said the worst

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:21.269
question I have is “What have you been in
big lately that I’ve seen?”

00:14:21.269 --> 00:14:25.009
And I said “Yes, that’s a terrible question,
too.

00:14:25.009 --> 00:14:26.009
So what is your answer?”

00:14:26.009 --> 00:14:28.550
And she goes “The Pacific Ocean.”

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:32.639
And I always loved that because it showed
that here’s a creative person who has figured

00:14:32.639 --> 00:14:39.380
out how to protect her mind from the inevitable
things that are going to happen in the big

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:40.380
world.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:41.380
People are not born with sensitivity.

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:45.450
They don’t walk out of their homes on a
way to a party going “I’m going to be

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:47.509
particularly sensitive today.”

00:14:47.509 --> 00:14:50.980
And the first thing they say to an actress
they meet is “What have you been in big

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:52.060
that I’ve seen?”

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:56.070
It’s not because they are mean or that they
are nasty people (but maybe they are).

00:14:56.070 --> 00:15:01.690
It’s probably because they aren’t being
sensitive and you having that answer instantly

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:07.089
bonds you with them and makes them respect
you for respecting yourself enough to not

00:15:07.089 --> 00:15:08.279
take their questions seriously.

00:15:08.279 --> 00:15:13.420
You don’t ever have to answer any question
that somebody gives you unless you feel like

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:18.759
it so when she answers it that way she disarms
the whole situation.

00:15:18.759 --> 00:15:23.440
Whereas the first girl is not doing such a
good job because she shouldn’t be going

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:28.639
to parties until she can answer that question
about going back to Arkansas and working for

00:15:28.639 --> 00:15:30.560
the Post Office.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:37.259
And that’s another example of protecting
your mind and not protecting your mind and

00:15:37.259 --> 00:15:40.089
having the introspection to know how to deal.

00:15:40.089 --> 00:15:47.319
And you were talking about whether people
react to your deciding to be creative.

00:15:47.319 --> 00:15:51.639
You know I always say it’s like there’s
this guy down the street who has been painting

00:15:51.639 --> 00:15:57.290
in his garage for the last ten years and the
neighbors are talking they’ve been talking

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:00.640
about him as if he’s a crackpot.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:03.500
He’s been doing that for 20 years whatever?

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:08.490
And then one day they read in the paper that
one of his paintings sold for a million dollars

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:09.740
and what do they say?

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:13.079
“I always knew the guy was a genius?

00:16:13.079 --> 00:16:15.889
He had to be a genius to work that hard.”

00:16:15.889 --> 00:16:21.710
But everything suddenly changes when the world
accepts your creativity.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:27.540
But the only way you are going to get to that
point is if you absolutely control what you’re

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:33.399
doing and believe in it yourself and even
if you don’t believe in it, keep acting

00:16:33.399 --> 00:16:34.399
as though you do.

00:16:34.399 --> 00:16:37.350
In other words you don’t have to believe
in things.

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:41.449
You don’t have to feel good in order to
work and you don’t have to feel good in

00:16:41.449 --> 00:16:43.589
order to do good work.

00:16:43.589 --> 00:16:49.250
You can work and normally when you work you
can get rid of these feelings anyway.

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:56.690
So this is all examples of dealing with the
creative mind and how to get it to be your

00:16:56.690 --> 00:17:01.380
friend as opposed to be something you are
scared of and don’t want to take off to

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:02.380
a cabin in the woods.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:09.380
Film Courage: Well I noticed with A STAR IS
BORN (which is up for an Oscar, we’re just

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:18.500
about a month away) what struck me about the
film was the loneliness of the creative process

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:24.890
and the lack of people around them when they
were working on things and whether it was

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:31.340
his drinking or whatever it was, but that
it was so lonely, It was just them and their

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:32.340
material.

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:36.520
Yes they had handlers and dancers around them
and different things but when they were home

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:38.090
it was very lonely.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:39.490
I though that was very interesting.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:44.780
Dr. Atchity: Yes, it’s the kind of loneliness
that you can’t really describe to people

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:48.450
who are not part of it.

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:51.880
So after awhile you stop trying to describe
it.

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:55.180
Maybe you go to a shrink and talk to the shrink
about it.

00:17:55.180 --> 00:18:00.290
One of my clients is a shrink for creative
people and probably half the people in Hollywood

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:02.100
business go to him.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:10.280
And they all have the same problems having
to do with the unbearable heaviness of what

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:16.300
they do and the fact that it is a lonely process
that no one understands.

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:18.990
Like I’m a producer and people say "What
does the producer do?”

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:25.810
And I have to give a Pacific Ocean kind of
answer to that because it is a long conversation

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:33.560
and nobody understands it and nobody’s really
that interested anyway, so it’s just that’s

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.570
what you are dealing with in the creative
world.

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:41.540
You are trying to articulate things that are
alien to most people who are not living creative

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:51.220
lives and it’s a burden to bear but it gets
easier to bear the more lightly you take it,

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:53.530
when you don’t take it that heavily.

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:58.320
When you have a dog or a cat or something
that makes you feel human.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:03.720
If you cook, like I love cooking and I love
playing tennis and I’m not thinking creative

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:05.660
thoughts when I’m cooking or playing tennis.

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:09.260
I’m just doing those things.

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:15.000
So I think you just have to give yourself
the chance to be with your own mind and figure

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:19.550
it out and realize that you can control it.

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:26.060
I always think the creative mind has these
parts to it that the artist really needs to

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:27.260
be aware of.

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:33.340
And the parts are there’s a great big bunch
of it if you imagine the mind like a big globe,

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:39.070
there’s a huge continent in the middle of
it that I call The Continent Of Reason.

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:42.240
And it is all of the established things in
your life.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:46.980
It’s your entire education, it’s your
ability to tell time and how many languages

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:48.780
you can tell time in and how many languages
you can tell it in.

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:53.810
It’s even language because if you weren’t
on that continent you wouldn’t need a language

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:54.810
right?

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:58.710
If you weren’t communicating with millions
of people you wouldn’t need languages.

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:03.080
So everything that’s orderly is from that
continent of reason.

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:09.670
And then there’s these islands all over
the place that are each individual and they

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:15.740
don’t have anything to do with the continent
and on those islands, strange things can happen.

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:23.570
I call those the visionary islands of a mind
and the most people are trained as they’re

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:29.480
growing up when their parent talked them out
of being a painter and talked them into being

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.400
a dentist you know?

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:39.000
Or talked them out of being a ballerina and
talked them into being a teller at the bank,

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:45.830
those people are trained to be members of
the continent, to be good members of the society

00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:51.570
that is the continent of reason where everything
is orderly, where you show up at nine o’clock,

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:54.420
you don’t show up at 9:05.

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:57.760
You know if you show up at quarter to nine,
that’s good.

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:01.970
But quarter after 9:00, that’s the end of
the job.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:08.220
So those people are raised that way and the
artist refuses to be raised that way.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:13.110
He wants to visit all of these islands and
he wants to somehow do something with those

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:20.380
islands and eventually he wants to introduce
those islands to the continent because it

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:25.930
takes stuff from the continent (like language)
in order to write a story.

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:36.280
It takes stuff from the continent like color
and lines and framing to be a painter.

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:44.150
And if you don’t know those basic conventions
you can’t be a painter.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:49.510
So you learn then but your goal as an artist
is to make them different than anything that’s

00:21:49.510 --> 00:21:52.500
ever been on the continent before right?

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:59.890
And eventually if you succeed and just as
jumping way ahead then what you’ve done

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:04.410
is now part of the continent if you’re succeeded.

00:22:04.410 --> 00:22:09.080
And I’ve never heard that put more eloquently
than in a brilliant little book called Picasso

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:12.650
by Gertrude Stein that everybody artistic
should read.

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:19.180
But one of the things she says in there is
that everybody thought that what Pablo was

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:26.000
seeing was different but he was only seeing
what he was seeing.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:29.380
He was not seeing what anyone else was seeing.

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:30.680
He was just seeing what he was seeing.

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:36.460
And after awhile he started painting what
he was seeing and only what he was seeing

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:43.200
and before long suddenly we were seeing what
he was seeing.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:49.060
And that kind of explains the whole process
by which an original vision gets translated

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:51.560
into a classic.

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:58.300
Picasso is now considered a classic painter
in terms of history of art and only because

00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:05.990
he saw things differently and had the courage
and strength to convey his vision and then

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:11.460
finally his vision started catching on because
somebody bought a napkin for a million dollars

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:21.770
you know and he was no longer the crazy painter
which he was absolutely before that commercial

00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:29.890
breath through and that’s part of the excitement
of it is to see how this changed culture by

00:23:29.890 --> 00:23:34.350
sticking to their eccentric sort of anti-cultural
stance.

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:43.500
We’re talking about how the artist in anti-cultural
in the beginning because he’s pursuing his

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:50.970
own private vision and when his private vision
begins to be accepted by the larger culture

00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:59.730
then he becomes an established artist and
that sounds good to the persons who live on

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:06.830
the continent of reason but to the artist
that becomes dangerous and fraught with peril

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:13.850
because he was never interested in being like
the people on the continent and now he is

00:24:13.850 --> 00:24:15.640
one of those people.

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:16.810
So what does he do?

00:24:16.810 --> 00:24:19.960
He goes through periods if he’s Picasso.

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:25.850
He starts writing different kinds of books
if he’s a writer and his publishers don’t

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:30.960
like that because they like him to write thrillers
because they’re part of the continent of

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:35.880
reason and the continent of reason invented
pigeon holes and niches.

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:40.011
You know “find your niche young man” someone
once told me “find your niche” because

00:24:40.011 --> 00:24:45.160
I was trying to do a magazine about dreams
and the arts and he was the editor of Psychology

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:47.510
Today and the word niche.

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:52.130
And that’s the continent of reason telling
you you are too far out there, it’s not

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:53.550
going to work.

00:24:53.550 --> 00:25:00.770
Okay, well we stubbornly continued (my co-editor
and I) and we created a magazine that lasted

00:25:00.770 --> 00:25:06.400
for ten years published in New York and so
on but only because we ignored him telling

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:08.290
us to find the niche.

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:12.860
When we found that niche we have to think
of “What are we going to do next?”

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:15.110
And that’s what Picasso has to think about.

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:17.410
So he switched to his blue period.

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:26.020
He switches to his cubist period and so on
just because he’s competing with himself.

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:32.710
Part of the culture is now earlier Picasso
and that is a tremendous burden for the successful

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:34.000
artist to bear.

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:38.030
Think about [Igor] Stravinsky whose greatest
were his first works.

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:44.640
But the guy lived to be 90 years old but the
Rite of Spring and Petrushka and The Firebird

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:48.400
Suite were all written when he was much younger.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:53.720
How does a guy like that lie through the next
40 years?

00:25:53.720 --> 00:26:01.620
With great difficulty and experimentation
and switching from composing to conducting

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:05.780
and lots of other things (it wasn’t like
he didn’t have a worthwhile life).

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:12.790
But he was always nagged and haunted that
his art in a sense was premature when it comes

00:26:12.790 --> 00:26:17.590
to healthy, happy mental development.

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:21.990
These are the kind of issues that artists
deal with and that’s why a lot of people

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.970
tell you “Don’t do that.

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:26.690
Just work fo the Post Office.”

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:28.520
Work in a secure position.

00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:29.560
Film Courage: It didn’t work for Bukowski.

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:32.060
Dr. Atchity: No, it didn’t.

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:38.190
He was a good friend when he was around and
talk about hovels.

00:26:38.190 --> 00:26:43.770
I once tool my five-year-old daughter to his
house to pick up something because he was

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:47.410
speaking at the poetry series at Occidental
College that I was in charge of.

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:52.280
And she walked into the house and she said
at the top of her voice “Dad, this is the

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:55.430
filthiest house I’ve ever seen in my life!”

00:26:55.430 --> 00:27:00.120
And it’s true, there was toilet paper on
the flow, there were dirty dishes all over

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:03.060
the floor, it was a mess.

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:07.280
But he wrote incredible poems that moved everybody.

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:11.930
When I went to Italy as a Fulbright professor
I was surprised to learn that rather than

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:16.081
Wallace Stevens and Hemingway (all of which
I was prepared to teach) and Melville, they

00:27:16.081 --> 00:27:18.290
only wanted to hear about Bukowski.

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:27.700
His books were translated into Italian (all
of them) and he was a mess and his personal

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:32.200
life was a mess and he kind of liked it that
way.

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:34.430
He never ran out of material to write.

00:27:34.430 --> 00:27:41.420
And he’s one of the first who was able to
sustain a long career without feeling trapped

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:43.260
by his previous career.

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:47.230
He was happy kind of doing what he was doing
over and over again.

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:52.650
I was talking about publishers wanting a writer
to constantly do thrillers because that’s

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:57.270
where his niche is and that’s where he should
do thrillers.

00:27:57.270 --> 00:28:03.190
But the writer might want to write romance
and the publishers are not interested.

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:04.190
Wait a minute?

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:08.430
We’ve made 6 million dollars out of you
as a thriller writers and I don’t know if

00:28:08.430 --> 00:28:10.420
you can even speak to the romance audience.

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:12.930
Well I’d like to try?

00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:16.150
Well okay, then we’re going to have to use
a different name.

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:23.040
So a typical response to an artist even like
Agatha Christie to have four or five pen names

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:25.380
and write under many names.

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:29.530
Also, Stephen King for example.

00:28:29.530 --> 00:28:35.970
Because they want to write different things,
they don’t want to be repetitive and force

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:41.740
their art into a mold as part of the continent
of reason.

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:46.640
I’ve always seen that those are the two
big things in the artist’s mind but then

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:50.650
there’s the third thing which I call the
managing editor which is part of the mind

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:52.480
which sees this whole thing.

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:58.370
It’s similar to meditators telling you that
there’s this third eye, this watcher that

00:28:58.370 --> 00:29:02.980
you have to develop to see and be thinking
to realize that it is not you.

00:29:02.980 --> 00:29:05.460
That there is more to you than just the thinking.

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:09.460
Well that’s kind of what we talk about in
one of my books.

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:13.890
The managing editor is the one who says I’ve
got to negotiate a deal between the continent

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:19.940
and the islands so that we can actually get
this book done because we need things from

00:29:19.940 --> 00:29:24.670
the continent like time which the continent
is in charge of because on the island there

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:33.381
is no time things happen all at once and there
is no beginning, middle and end, it’s just

00:29:33.381 --> 00:29:35.330
everything happens at once.

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:37.920
But on the continent that’s not allowed.

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:41.940
Things have to have a beginning, middle and
end in that order.

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:46.020
Unlike the Italian director who said that
a movie did have to have a beginning middle

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:50.410
and end but not necessarily in that order.

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:54.240
He was giving an island response to a continent
question.

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:57.390
And the question was does a movie have to
have a beginning, middle and end?

00:29:57.390 --> 00:30:00.610
He goes “Yes, but not in that order.”

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:06.090
And the managing editor is the part of your
mind that sees this and goes okay we’re

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:07.090
going to negotiate.

00:30:07.090 --> 00:30:11.960
If you say I am going to this cabin to write
this book no matter how much it takes, I’m

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:14.410
going to stay there until it is done.

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:18.990
The continent freaks out because it’s going
“Well I’m going to starve to death.

00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:22.190
What’s going to happen if you never finish
the book?”

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:27.410
But the managing editor works out a deal and
goes “We are only going to do two hours

00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:32.090
a day, three hours a day and we are only going
to do it for ten weeks.”

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:37.530
And at the end of that with that many hours,
it would be done and here’s the path.

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:44.690
So the continent is allowed to relax because
this intervening force is told this crazy

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:49.801
island wants to write this book, you guys
can come out and do this but you are going

00:30:49.801 --> 00:30:53.470
to have this much time and blah, blah, blah,
in this compartment.

00:30:53.470 --> 00:30:59.851
That’s what I think makes the sane artist
as opposed to someone who is not sane who

00:30:59.851 --> 00:31:02.030
is working out deals like that like with themselves.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:06.370
Maybe not so formally but that’s what they
do.

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:09.170
They make bargains to keep their art going.

00:31:09.170 --> 00:31:14.460
Film Courage: Well in the case of [Charles]
Bukowski the sort of “slavery” of his

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:18.960
9-to-5 job (if you want to call it that) was
the impetus for a lot of his stories and it

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:24.050
helped fuel him and give him that chip on
his shoulder and put a voice to what so many

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:25.470
people felt.

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:28.910
So in that case, it’s as if it worked for
him.

00:31:28.910 --> 00:31:34.250
Dr. Atchity: Yes and Wallace Stevens who was
one of my favorite American poets and oddly

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:40.600
similar to Bukowski in very interesting ways,
was selling insurance all of his life.

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:45.910
He was writing his greatest poem like Sunday
Morning on a train commuting from Hartford

00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:52.210
to New Haven wearing a three piece suit because
he was an insurance salesman and that’s

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:53.510
what he had to do.

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:54.510
T.S.

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:59.620
Elliot was working as a bank teller when he
was writing The Wasteland.

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:06.620
So yes, ordinary jobs can be used to spark
creativity.

00:32:06.620 --> 00:32:11.370
And the artist like Bukowski in his later
years found himself more and more troubled

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:17.340
when he had an unstructured life and didn’t
have to go anywhere.

